-- title:
--   The integer lattice is closed under differences
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathbb{Z}^d \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ be the integer lattice, the set of points all of whose coordinates are integers. If $x \in \mathbb{Z}^d$ and $y \in \mathbb{Z}^d$, then
--   $$x - y \;\in\; \mathbb{Z}^d .$$
--
--   The lattice is defined here as a subset of $\mathbb{R}^d$ cut out by a coordinatewise condition rather than as a subgroup object, so its closure under subtraction has to be recorded explicitly; it is what allows displacements between lattice sites to be treated as lattice vectors, as every translation-invariance argument on the lattice requires.
